WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.980
Along with big-budget disaster flicks and
edgy rom-coms, thrillers with a strong female

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.220
lead were a huge part of the cinema landscape
in the 1990s.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.800
Whether it was Sandra Bullock tracking down
conspiracies in The Net or Julia Roberts escaping

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:17.090
an abusive spouse in Sleeping with the Enemy,
audiences were eager to see women taking on

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.160
nefarious men.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.380
Few films personified this trend as well as
the Ashley-Judd-and-Tommy-Lee-Jones-starring

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.030
revenge tale Double Jeopardy.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.160
Beware: there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.160
In the film, Judd plays the quiet and kind
Libby Parsons, whose wealthy husband Nick

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.100
vanishes while they're alone on a boat.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.040
Although Libby is clueless as to what's happening,
she's eventually convicted of Nick's murder

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.260
and sent to prison.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.000
But, plot twist, Nick is very much alive,
and he purposefully framed Libby for his death.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.100
After one of her fellow inmates clues her
into the legal concept of double jeopardy,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.899
the idea that someone can't be tried twice
for the same crime, Libby vows to use this

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:53.750
loophole to make sure Nick doesn't get the
chance to enjoy the new life he's created

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.520
for himself.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.540
The plan: She'll falsely admit to murdering
Nick in order to be released from prison on

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.640
good behavior, then kill Nick for real, as
she's already been tried and convicted for

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.060
that particular crime.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.800
While the core idea of Double Jeopardy is,
well, not exactly legally airtight, who really

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.560
cares?

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.060
The movie is an enjoyable slice of late '90s
thriller fun, which is probably why it's currently

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.560
on the list of top 10 movies on Netflix.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.940
"Kinda makes you feel warm and tingly all
over, don't it?"

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.580
But back when it came out, the movie took
a beating from critics.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.800
The late Roger Ebert was more charitable than
many in his two-and-a-half star review, though

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.060
he still called the film,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.270
"...not a successful thriller, but with some
nice dramatic scenes along with the dumb mystery

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.400
and contrived conclusion."

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.320
Entertainment Weekly described the movie as
"toothless" and "a strained reworking of The

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:39.920
Fugitive."

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:40.900
Reel Views wrote,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.000
"Unfortunately, this movie is too focused
on action, melodrama, and plot twists to allow

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.350
for more than a moment's intelligence to worm
its way into the script."

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.440
Despite the middling reviews, the movie was
a hit with audiences.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.260
It spent several weeks at number 1 at the
US box office, and it raked in nearly $178

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.860
million across the globe.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.240
Considering its current popularity on Netflix,
it's clear that something about Double Jeopardy

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.760
has always connected with the general public,
despite the silly plot.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.480
Reviews credited Ashley Judd for her performance.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.660
Though Reel Views critiqued the movie as a
whole, they did lavish praise on Judd's performance,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.260
saying,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.400
"Ashley Judd almost makes Libby credible.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.519
In fact, the actress is so good that, against
all odds, we like the character even though

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:22.329
she only uses about 1% of her brain cells."

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:26.260
Double Jeopardy has gone on to become one
of Judd's most noteworthy roles, but her part

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.420
in the film originally went to another actress.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.440
According to an interview in Urban Cinefile
with the film's director, Jodie Foster was

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.580
originally attached.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.440
He explained,

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.919
"She fell out of the project, because she
got pregnant, and we had to recast.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.740
That's how Ashley Judd wound up in it."

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.659
Second choice or not, the movie helped cement
Judd's status as a go-to star for thrillers

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.180
with tough women at their center.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.440
Before Double Jeopardy, she starred in Kiss
the Girls, along with Morgan Freeman, a film

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.959
based on a popular James Patterson novel which
sees Freeman's Detective Alex Cross on the

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.100
hunt for a serial killer named Casanova.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.480
Judd plays a would-be victim of Casanova who
manages to escape, and then partners with

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.220
Cross to help him track down the killer.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.340
After Double Jeopardy, Judd teamed up with
Freeman again for High Crimes, where she plays

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.500
a lawyer who finds out her husband has been
hiding his participation in war crimes.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.580
And following that, she starred in Twisted
as a San Francisco police officer with a drinking

00:03:14.589 --> 00:03:18.640
problem who finds herself at the center of
a conspiracy when her former lovers keep turning

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.360
up murdered.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.360
Unfortunately, Twisted is also notorious for
having a one percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.499
But clearly, even over two decades since the
release of Double Jeopardy, audiences are

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.880
still connecting with her portrayal of a woman
hell-bent on revenge.

